  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unusually warm weather this afternoon and \nWednesday, with forecast temperatures potentially reaching \nrecord highs for early November.\n\nTemperatures already around 70F at KIAG and KROC. Record is 75F\nat KROC so that has a shot to be broken.  \n\nA mid-level ridge will continue to build east across the Atlantic \nseaboard through mid-week, supporting surface high pressure to \npersist off the eastern Atlantic. Overall this will support \ngenerally dry weather. The warm front has lifted through most of\nthe western and central areas, and should reach the east by\nevenings. In the wake of the front, dry air advection and \nsubsidence will support a decrease in cloud cover from west to \neast through tonight. A 25 to 30 knot low- level jet will move \ninto the western Southern Tier tonight, supporting a south- \nsoutheast surface wind within a modest inversion. As such, gusty\ndownslope winds of up to 30mph along and to the lee side of the\nChautauqua Ridge is likely. Gusts will lessen on the lee side \nof the Ridge Wednesday.\n\nAs the upper ridge builds further north Wednesday, 850mb \ntemperatures will continue to rise, peaking to +13C. As such \nanother very warm day for early November is expected, where \nmaximum temperatures will range in the mid to upper 70s. As \nwinds are forecast to remain southerly, lake breeze influences \nwill likely be limited and therefor allow temperatures to soar \nwell above climatological norms. See Climate section for more \ninformation regarding record temperatures. \n\nCooler air will build into the region from west to east Thursday, \nhowever it will remain warm especially from the Finger Lakes region \ninto north-central NY. Cooler weather, although still above normal \nis expected Friday into the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High probability for widespread showers Wednesday \nnight through Thursday due to the passage of a cold front. Unsettled \nweather is expected to continue into the weekend.  \n\nDeep troughing and associated closed low will move into the Great \nLakes for the later half of the work week. A deep southerly flow \noriginating from the Gulf and southern Atlantic Ocean will support a \nplume of moisture into the eastern Great Lakes region Wednesday \nnight through Thursday morning. This deep moisture combined with \nforcing from the surface to 500mb due to a passing cold front will \nsupport widespread showers across the region, and provide some much \nneeded relief to the recent dry spell. However should note that the \nshower's residence timing will be quick, as the plume of \nmoisture will move east of the region by Thursday afternoon. \nForecast rainfall amounts will generally be less than a quarter \nof an inch by Thursday afternoon.\n\nIn the wake of the cold front, expect gusty southwest winds \nnortheast of Lake Erie with gusts of 25 to 30 mph impacting much of \nthe Niagara Frontier. \n\nThe trough will move overhead Friday and this weekend, where another \ncold front is progged to pass across the region, where chances for \nshowers are possible.",
